# Google Play (Trusted Web Activity)

`android/twa-manifest.json` is the Bubblewrap configuration of
`__PACKAGE_ID__`, which opens `https://__HOST__/` full screen.

1. Deploy the site over HTTPS at `https://__HOST__/`.
2. Install Bubblewrap: `npm i -g @bubblewrap/cli` (Node.js 18 or later and
   JDK 17; it offers to install the Android command-line tools on first run).
3. Create the upload key once, outside the repository, and let `keytool`
   prompt for the passwords:

   ```bash
   keytool -genkeypair -keystore /private/path/upload.keystore \
     -alias upload -keyalg RSA -keysize 3072 -validity 10000
   ```

   Put its real path and alias in `signingKey` in `twa-manifest.json`, and
   back the keystore up: every update of the app must be signed with it.
4. In `android/`, `bubblewrap update --skipVersionUpgrade` generates the
   Android project from `twa-manifest.json`, and `bubblewrap build` writes the
   signed `app-release-bundle.aab`. For later releases, `bubblewrap update`
   (without the flag) raises `appVersionCode` before `bubblewrap build`.
5. Upload the bundle in Play Console. With Play App Signing, Google re-signs
   the app: copy the SHA-256 fingerprint of the **app signing key** from the
   App integrity page (add your upload key's too for builds you install
   yourself).
6. Publish the fingerprints, restart the application and open
   `https://__HOST__/.well-known/assetlinks.json`:

   ```bash
   RULLST_ANDROID_ASSET_LINKS="__PACKAGE_ID__=<app signing SHA-256>,<upload SHA-256>"
   ```

   or in `Rullst.toml`:

   ```toml
   [[android.asset_links]]
   package_name = "__PACKAGE_ID__"
   sha256_cert_fingerprints = ["<app signing SHA-256>"]
   ```

Without a valid file Android still opens the app, but with a browser address
bar. Web changes reach installed apps without a new bundle.
